<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wusun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE–5th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wusun were a nomadic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> known from Chinese sources. Displaced from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> region, they drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> from the Ili valley around 130 BCE and ruled the steppes of Zhetysu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> for several centuries, absorbing the Saka who remained there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> courted them as allies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and sent Chinese princesses to marry their rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wusun.jpg" alt="Wusun" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derzsi Elekes Andor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:30._Rider_burial_mound_Tenlik_(III.-II._B.C.)_Kazakhstan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Wusun were a semi-nomadic steppe people named in Chinese records from the 2nd century BCE to the 5th century CE. They first appear living near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains">Qilian Mountains</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. Chinese sources tell that the Yuezhi attacked them and killed their king Nandoumi. By legend his infant son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liejiaomi">Liejiaomi</a> was left in the wild, suckled by a she-wolf and fed meat by ravens. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> ruler adopted the child, made him leader of the Wusun and a general, and Liejiaomi won many victories for the Xiongnu.</p><p>The origin of the name is debated, and scholars have linked it to the Asmiraioi of Ptolemy's Geography, to words for horse and to a word for tent. Most scholars take the Wusun to have spoken an Indo-European language, probably Iranian, though Tocharian and Indo-Aryan have also been proposed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> permission <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liejiaomi">Liejiaomi</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> in the Ili Valley in the 2nd century BCE, and the Yuezhi migrated to Sogdia and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. The Wusun took over the valley, and when the Xiongnu ruler died Liejiaomi refused to serve them. In 125 BCE the Han diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> was sent to seek an alliance against the Xiongnu and estimated the Wusun at 630,000 people. Their court was the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigu">Chigu</a>, near Issyk Kul, whose exact site is unknown.</p><p>The Wusun at first hesitated from fear of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, but after Han blows against the Xiongnu and a threat of war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liejiaomi">Liejiaomi</a> sent horses and accepted the Han princess Xijun as a wife. A Xiongnu princess was declared his senior consort. After Xijun's early death, Princess Jieyou succeeded her and lived among the Wusun for fifty years. In 72 BCE the Wusun requested Han help against the Xiongnu, and a Han army of 160,000 inflicted a crushing defeat on them. Afterwards the Wusun gained significant influence over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. In the 5th century CE, pressed by the Rouran, they may have moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamir Mountains</a>; they were last mentioned in Chinese sources in 436 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Wusun were governed by a hereditary Great Kunmi, assisted by a council of elders and an administration of sixteen officials, and every freeman counted as a warrior. Their society was highly stratified, by ownership of property; the wealthiest are believed to have owned 4,000 to 5,000 horses. Slaves, mostly prisoners of war, worked as servants and craftsmen.</p><p>Early Chinese descriptions record that they lived in felt tents, ate meat and drank fermented mare's milk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a>-raising was their main activity, but they also practised agriculture. The Kargali burial of a female shaman near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a> held nearly 300 gold objects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wusun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pushed west by the Xiongnu and the <b>Wusun</b>, the Yuezhi reached the land north of the Oxus, and around 140 BC the Saka began to invade Parthia and Bactria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Wusun</b> in turn expelled the Yuezhi, the Wusun state absorbed the remaining Saka of the Ili region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>There they were attacked by the <b>Wusun</b> under their ruler, the kunmo, whose father had been killed, according to the Han shu, by the Yuezhi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Military</a></li></ul></section>
